{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>Your name is Jacob; no longer shall your name be called Jacob.</b> Since it says \"Your name is Jacob,\" the implication is that the name Jacob remains in its place and is not uprooted — and afterward it says, \"no longer shall your name be called Jacob.\" Rather, it surely means not that the name Jacob would be uprooted, but that it would be secondary, etc. But concerning Abraham, our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Berakhot 13a) that anyone who calls Abraham 'Abram' transgresses a negative commandment — and the reason is that concerning him it was not said, \"Your name is Abram,\" as it is said here, \"Your name is Jacob.\"" "And by way of allusion, there is room for me to explain his two names of Jacob after the two redemptions, for we find that our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Jeremiah 23:7–8) — not that the Exodus from Egypt would be uprooted entirely, but that the mention of the miracles of the Exodus from Egypt would be secondary, and the memory of the miracles of the future would be primary. And so here they said (Genesis Rabbah 78:3): not that the name Jacob would be uprooted entirely, but that it would be secondary and the name Israel primary. If so, both are one matter. And thus it says concerning the final redemption (Isaiah 40:4): ״וְהָיָה הֶעָקֹב לְמִישׁוֹר״ — עָקֹב is derived from the name יַעֲקֹב, and מִישׁוֹר is derived from the name יִשְׂרָאֵל יָשָׁר אֵל. And since it says (Micah 7:15) ״כִּימֵי צֵאתְךָ מֵאֶרֶץ מִצְרַיִם אַרְאֶנּוּ נִפְלָאוֹת״, infer from this that the miracles of the future will not be greater than the miracles of Egypt — so why would the miracles of Egypt be secondary? Rather, because in the redemption from Egypt their merit was not sufficient to bring them out בְּיָד רָמָה, and they needed to go בְּעָקְבָה וּרְמִיָּה — for they said ״נֵלְכָה לִזְבֹּחַ לַה׳ אֱלֹהֵינוּ״ (Exodus 5:3), and every fugitive goes בְּחִפָּזוֹן, as it is written (Exodus 12:11) ״וַאֲכַלְתֶּם אֹתוֹ בְּחִפָּזוֹן״. And the redemption of Babylon was plainly not a complete redemption but merely a visitation, and there too it says (Isaiah 48:20) ״צְאוּ מִבָּבֶל בִּרְחוּ מִכַּשְׂדִּים״." "But in the final redemption, after the sin has been purged completely through the length of this bitter exile, their merit will suffice that they go out בְּיָד רָמָה בְּמִישׁוֹר וְלֹא בְּעָקְבָה וְלֹא בְּחִפָּזוֹן, as it is written (Isaiah 52:12) ״כִּי לֹא בְחִפָּזוֹן תֵּצֵאוּ וּבִמְנוּסָה לֹא תֵלֵכוּן״ — this is what it says, ״וְהָיָה הֶעָקֹב לְמִישׁוֹר״. Nevertheless the name Jacob is not uprooted entirely, and likewise the memory of the Exodus from Egypt is not uprooted entirely, so that they may remember both redemptions together and know and recognize the difference between them which sin caused — for without doubt they were in Egypt wicked and sinful, as is explained in the prophecy of Ezekiel (Ezekiel 20:8), and from this side it is fitting that it be secondary; and likewise the name Jacob is not uprooted entirely for this reason. Moreover, the redemption serves two redemptions: the redemption of the bodies from servitude and afflictions, and the redemption of the soul from the side of the spiritual matters that were given to Israel — corresponding to the indication of the names Jacob and Israel, one material and one spiritual, as found in Rabbenu Bachye. And even though the spiritual is primary, nevertheless the name Jacob is not uprooted entirely, for a person needs to give a portion to this and to that. But the name Abram is uprooted entirely, for in all generations he is always Av Hamon Goyim, for the tents of Edom and Ishmael too issue from his loins."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 35, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:32:15.502961602Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}